Actually, the characteristic "quantum" systems have in common seems to be 
low *entropy*. As soon as a quantum system gets entangled with a system 
with a huge number of degrees of freedom it seems to act classical, so it 
seems likely to me that "collapse" is actually some sort of thermodynamics 
at play. An uncollapsed quantum system in an indeterminate state is very 
delicate -- suggesting that like a china vase, it's some sort of 
remarkably low entropy state very easy to disrupt and difficult to create. 
Also, this has the nice and tidy effect of tying together all of the so-
called arrows of time. There's four unidirectional processes observed, 
despire the apparent time reversal symmetry of fundamental laws. One is 
the forward flow of human experience; another is cosmological, a big bang 
in one direction and an accelerating expansion in the other; there's the 
thermodynamic arrow, in which things break, evolve, etc. in a preferred 
time direction; and there's the quantum wave collapse. The first three are 
easy to show are aspects of one thing: human experience is the 
accumulation of memories, which requires increasing information (entropy) 
content, and so is tied to the thermodynamic arrow and is really a 
particular manifestation of it. The thermodynamic arrow is in turn a 
consequence of the cosmic arrow. Relativity theory requires the universe 
have one of two shapes -- either it's a "cone" or a "football" in time, 
the former having a bang/crunch at one end and the latter at both. The 
determinant is whether it exceeds the critical density, which it either 
does in all spacelike volumes or in none. There's at least one temporal 
"end" though near which the universe is very small, and quantum theory 
requires it to have very few degrees of freedom then, and thus low 
entropy. Thus, there is a low entropy boundary condition at each end of 
the "football" or the pointy end of the "cone", and thermodynamics says 
the tendency is for entropy to increase away from such a boundary 
condition, so the thermodynamic (and hence psychological) arrow points 
away from the nearest "end" and observers must necessarily find that a Big 
Bang exists in their past (rather than an infinite past with a finite 
future and then a crunch -- that's a valid relativistic cosmology, but 
it's "the film run backwards" as any observer will be accreting memories 
in the *other direction*!). (We happen to find every indication there is 
no Crunch in the future. It may be *necessarily* so, as it seems there'd 
otherwise be paradoxes of time reversal at the midpoint!)

The quantum collapse arrow was the odd one out, until I realized that 
"collapse" seems in empirical practise to mean "the wavefunction got 
entangled with a large, noisy, real-world system with high entropy" and 
figured that it must also be the thermodynamic arrow in disguise...

